            .cards {
                display: grid;
                grid-template-columns: 2fr 1fr;
                gap: 1em 2em;
            }

            /* Each card is a row of the grid, so evidence and conclusion line up across cards */
            .card {
                display: grid;
                grid-template-columns: subgrid;
                grid-column: 1 / -1;
                align-items: center;
            }
            blockquote {
                font-size: .6em;
            }

            /* The figure is drawn at its usual size and shrunk; a transform does not change
               the layout box, so the wrapper takes the shrunk size */
            .thumbnail {
                width: 600px;
                height: 400px;
                margin: 0 auto;
            }
            .thumbnail x-ratings {
                width: 600px;
                height: 400px;
                transform: scale(0.9);
                transform-origin: top left;
            }

            /* Red frame around the emphasized cells of the ratings. Step 1: ease of playing of the
               Test violin; step 3 (second quote shown): its tone instead */
            .card:first-child .evidence.visible x-ratings::part(Klimke-ease),
            .cards:has(.card:nth-child(2) .evidence.visible) x-ratings::part(Klimke-tone) {
                stroke-opacity: 1;
            }
            .cards:has(.card:nth-child(2) .evidence.visible) x-ratings::part(Klimke-ease) {
                stroke-opacity: 0;
            }
        }


    aside(data-markdown).notes.
        We propose two mechanisms behind this change in the Test Violinist's perception:
        On the one hand, an expectation bias could explain why the change of the Test Violin was perceived as positive. Our Test Violinist knows about and believes in the playing-in effect. We can therefore hypothesise that her expectations about the playing period biased her perception.
        On the other hand, since she played this violin regularly for 6 months, it is reasonable to think that she adapted to the instrument. According to our results, this adaptation did not involve any audible change in playing technique (H2). It could, however, go through micro-adjustments -- how she holds the violin, ease in shifting -- that reduce the perceived effort without changing the sound produced (H3).

section
    h3 Limitations
    ul
        li A single Test Violin / a single Test Violinist
        li Natural evolution

    aside(data-markdown).notes.
        Of course, our experiment has limitations:
        Our results only hold for the violins and violinists we observed. In particular, one could discuss the choice of the Test Violinist and of the Test Violin. So let's discuss it: the violinist was contacted after several people at the Conservatoire, as well as a "renowned" luthier, agreed that she was "the best person to open up a violin". When she agreed to take part in the experiment, we had her try 3 instruments from the Conservatoire's collection, all from the same period and very rarely played. The Test Violinist herself chose the Test Violin among these three, for its "potential to open up". Our sample is small, but not chosen at random.
        Also, the three violins did not evolve in quite the same conditions: while the two Control Violins were stored in the CNSM's instrument collection, the Test Violin was being played by the Test Violinist. Our results show, however, that the total change of the Test Violin -- which includes a possible playing-in effect on top of its natural evolution -- is of the same order of magnitude as the natural evolution of the Control Violins.

section
    p The burden of proof should be reversed

    aside(data-markdown).notes.
        Finally, even with these two limitations in mind, we think this study still challenges the beliefs around playing-in. When violinists and luthiers talk about this effect, they rarely qualify it: it is supposed to be observable on any violin, whoever the violinist.

        Our study, together with the recent literature, makes us think that a playing-in effect is unlikely to be explained by a material change of the instrument (H1). We also think that the burden of proof should be reversed: it is up to those convinced that the effect exists to show it.